<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Privacy manifest.

         WARNING: never write a double hyphen inside an XML comment.
         XML forbids it, so a file containing e.g. the literal flag
         "altool [two hyphens]validate-app" is not well formed even though
         `plutil -lint` still reports OK. Apple's validator is strict and
         rejects it with ITMS-91056. This exact mistake cost Pixling 1.0
         build 2 a rejection.

         Also, for reference, build 1 was rejected because this file had been
         written as JSON. It must be a plist (XML): Apple's docs render the
         keys in a JSON-ish style that is easy to copy verbatim, and a JSON
         file still builds and still passes altool validation before being
         rejected after submission.

         Verify BOTH the source and the packaged copy before uploading, and
         use a strict XML parser, not only plutil:
             plutil -lint Sources/Support/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy
             python3 -c "import plistlib;plistlib.load(open('Sources/Support/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy','rb'))"
         plutil passing is necessary but NOT sufficient.

         This app: zero networking, zero data collection. The only Required
         Reason API is UserDefaults (pets / coins / room layout).
         CA92.1 = access info from the same app. -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
